# 4. FEATURE A — REAL-TIME FACIAL ANALYSIS

Implement a working inference pipeline that supports both a webcam and prerecorded video files.

### Facial landmarks

Track facial landmarks needed for the eyes, mouth, and approximate head pose.

Handle:
- No face detected.
- Face temporarily moving out of the frame.
- Missing or unreliable landmarks.
- Multiple faces, using a documented and consistent subject-selection rule.
- Camera initialization failures.
- Video ending or becoming unavailable.
- Lighting changes and temporary tracking interruptions.

### Eye Aspect Ratio (EAR)

Calculate the Eye Aspect Ratio using an appropriate set of facial landmarks.

Use this metric to estimate whether the eye opening has decreased relative to its normal geometry.

### Mouth Aspect Ratio (MAR)

Calculate MAR to help identify sustained mouth opening and possible yawning events.

Require temporal evidence rather than reporting a yawn from one frame.

### Head pose

Estimate approximate yaw and pitch with OpenCV solvePnP and documented facial reference points.

Establish the neutral head pose during calibration. Detect sustained deviations rather than every brief head turn.

Head-pose deviation is only a potential distraction indicator; it does not establish where the driver is looking or prove that the driver is distracted.

### Blink tracking

Detect blink events and calculate blinks per minute over a configurable sliding window.

Prevent one blink from being counted repeatedly across consecutive frames.

### Visual output

Draw facial landmarks and display relevant metrics on the local monitoring window when useful.

Make all metric calculations reusable by the temporal engine, calibration module, evaluation script, and dashboard.

